                    })}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}

      <h3 class="sub">Typography · grouped by intention</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        There are <b>zero</b> tier-2 typography <em>tokens</em>. The layer exists as the composable
        classes in <code>type-roles.css</code> — composites of the tier-1 typography primitives, in
        exactly the shape the rubric describes for tier 2, just not expressed as tokens. Because they
        are classes, Figma can&rsquo;t consume them and a spoke can&rsquo;t re-point them by overriding
        a token. All {t2.typeCoverage.roles} roles are shown: {t2.typeCoverage.placed} across the six
        intentions, {t2.typeCoverage.outliers} outside them.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-note">
        The six property columns are fixed, so an unset property is visible as a hole rather than an
        absence. Two of the six are opt-in by design — <code>font-family</code> unset means
        &ldquo;the body face&rdquo; and <code>text-transform</code> unset means <code>none</code> — so
        those read <span class="dim">unset</span> quietly. The other four must be pinned for the role
        to be a complete composite; missing one is marked <span class="req">required</span>.
      </p>

      {[...t2.typeIntentions, { label: 'Outside the six', definition: t2.typeOutliers.note, roles: t2.typeOutliers.roles, note: undefined }].map((intent) => (
        <details class="tok-category prop type" data-group open>
          <summary>
            <span class="cat-label">{intent.label}</span>
            <span class="summary-right">
              {intent.note && <span class="inf-flag">inconsistent</span>}
              <span class="group-count">{intent.roles.length}</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="cat-note">{intent.definition}</p>
          {intent.note && <p class="cat-gap">{intent.note}</p>}
          <div class="cat-body">
            <table class="tok-table type-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Role</th>
                  {t2.TYPE_PROPS.map((p) => <th>{p}</th>)}
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {intent.roles.map((role) => (
                  <tr data-token={`.${role.className}`}>
                    <td class="c-name"><code>.{role.className}</code></td>
                    {t2.TYPE_PROPS.map((p) => {
                      const d = role.props[p];
                      if (!d) {
                        const required = (t2.REQUIRED_TYPE_PROPS as readonly string[]).includes(p);
                        return (
                          <td class={required ? 'unset is-required' : 'unset'}>
                            <span class={required ? 'req' : 'dim'}>{required ? 'required' : 'unset'}</span>
                          </td>
                        );
                      }
                      if (d.literal) return <td><code class="vocab warnv">{d.resolved}</code></td>;
                      return (
                        <td>
                          <code class="vocab">{d.ref}</code>
                          {d.fallbackRef && (
                            <span class="fb">↳ fallback <code>{d.fallbackRef}</code></span>
                          )}
                          <span class="dim resolved">{d.resolved ?? '?'}</span>
                        </td>
                      );
                    })}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}

      {t2.typeCoverage.incomplete.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-gap">
          <b>Incomplete composites:</b>{' '}
          {t2.typeCoverage.incomplete.map((r, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && ', '}
              <code>.{r.className}</code> (no {r.missing.join(', ')})
            </>
          ))}
          . A composite is meant to fully determine how text renders. Where a required property is
          unset the role inherits it instead, so the same class renders differently depending on what
          it sits inside — the exact failure a composite exists to prevent.
        </p>
      )}

      <h3 class="sub">Distance to the reference model</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        A worked example of the target <b>shape</b>, for calibration only —
        {' '}<b>{t2.referenceGap.refTotal}</b> composites named
        {' '}<code>&lt;intention&gt;-&lt;size&gt;[-mobile]</code>, each declaring all six properties,
        every value a reference with no literals, and each <code>-mobile</code> variant aliasing its
        desktop sibling for whatever doesn&rsquo;t change.
      </p>
      <p class="dev-banner">
        <strong>Structure, not values.</strong> The reference&rsquo;s px numbers are illustrative —
        they are not a scale we are trying to hit, and nothing on this page measures ours against
        them. What is being compared is which roles exist, which slots the names carry, whether a
        composite is complete, and whether a mobile/desktop pair is expressible.
      </p>

      <div class="tally">
        <div class="tally-cell t2"><b>{t2.referenceGap.covered}</b><span>of {t2.referenceGap.refDesktop} desktop roles have an equivalent</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t2.referenceGap.missing.length}</b><span>desktop roles with no equivalent</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell ok"><b>{t2.referenceGap.refMobile}</b><span>-mobile roles · ours live inside the clamp</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t2.referenceGap.extra.length}</b><span>of ours with no reference slot</span></div>
      </div>

      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>The clamp point.</b> Our sizes are fluid, but <code>clamp(min, fluid, max)</code> already
        carries the two discrete values the reference splits into two named roles — the <b>min is the
        mobile size</b> and the <b>max is the desktop size</b>. Every one of our roles therefore
        already <em>has</em> a desktop/mobile pair; it has just never been named as one. Exporting to
        Figma is a build-time extraction of the bounds, not a re-authoring. What we genuinely
        can&rsquo;t do is move the mobile value independently of the desktop one — the interpolation
        ties them together, so a <code>-mobile</code> role that changes letter-spacing has no
        equivalent here.
      </p>

      <table class="findings cov">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Reference role</th><th>-mobile sibling</th>
            <th>Our equivalent</th><th>size token</th><th>mobile/desktop pair</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.coverageByIntention.map((grp) => (
            <>
              <tr class="cov-head"><td colspan="5">{grp.intention}</td></tr>
              {grp.cells.map((c) => (
                <tr class={c.ours ? undefined : 'cov-miss'}>
                  <td><code>{c.reference.name}</code></td>
                  <td>
                    {c.mobile ? (
                      <span class="dim">declares {c.mobile.aliases.length ? `${6 - c.mobile.aliases.length} of 6, aliases the rest` : 'all 6'}</span>
                    ) : (
                      <span class="dim">none</span>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td>{c.ours ? <code>.{c.ours}</code> : <span class="req">none</span>}</td>
                  <td>{c.oursToken ? <code class="vocab">{c.oursToken}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                  <td>
                    {c.oursBounds ? (
                      <span class="pair">encoded in the clamp</span>
                    ) : (
                      <span class="dim">—</span>
                    )}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Structure only — the reference&rsquo;s px values are a worked example, not a target, so
        nothing here compares our scale against theirs. What the last column says is that wherever we
        have a role, the mobile/desktop pair already exists inside its clamp; it has never been given
        a name.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">Where the distance actually is</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        <span class="slot-flag f-absent">gap</span> is something the reference has and we lack.
        {' '}<span class="slot-flag f-partial">divergence</span> is a different decision with a
        rationale already written down in this repo — closing it would be a trade, not a fix.
